Below are the integrity rules for library loan records and one batch of 10 records to be checked against them. Either every rule holds or exactly one is broken, at one spot. Nothing is a violation unless a listed rule, taken literally, forbids it.

Each record has these fields: loan_id (text), card (text), slip_no (whole number), days_late (whole number), due_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), renewal_of (text), fine (whole number), renewals (whole number), condition_out (text), copy (text), branch (text), kiosk (text), borrowed_on (date, YYYY-MM-DD), reserve_code (text), desk (text), reserved (text), daily_fee (whole number).

Rules to check:
R1. No record has a `due_on` on or before its `borrowed_on`.
R2. `fine` is the product of `days_late` and `daily_fee`; any other stored value breaks the rule.
R3. `slip_no` rises by exactly 1 from each record to the next one in the batch; the first record may carry any number.
R4. A record whose `reserved` is "yes" needs a filled-in `reserve_code`; for other records `reserve_code` is optional.
R5. `renewals` stays within 0 to 5, limits included.
R6. Each `branch` has to appear in the reference list `branches` with the status "approved"; an entry with any other status does not count.
R7. Accepted values of `condition_out`: "worn", "new", "damaged"; nothing else.
R8. A filled-in `renewal_of` names the `loan_id` of another record of this batch, and that record comes before the one that names it.
R9. No two records agree in both `copy` and `card`; agreeing in just one of the two is fine.

Reference list `branches` with 7 entries (key: status): BR-23: revoked; BR-44: approved; BR-45: revoked; BR-50: approved; BR-59: approved; BR-76: approved; BR-80: pending

Note on statuses: a plain look-up rule (one that names no status) accepts any key of the list, with any status; a status is required only by a rule that names it.

